  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present. Some appendix table references are rendered as 'Table ??', but this does not prevent reading the reported results.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses activation steering as the basis for its own moral-vector defense, and reports safety and helpfulness outcomes. It does not test the original harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, its ablation, or whether that direction mediates refusal.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites the original refusal-direction study and discusses a monolithic refusal vector, but its vectors are extracted from five Moral Foundations Theory virtue-versus-vice categories. Its probing and generation results measure moral-category classification or virtue/vice output, not the refusal direction or refusal behavior described by V1. AMF safety results and capability benchmarks are recorded as related findings under fits_no_version. Cross-lingual probe accuracies are not recorded as rows because they measure moral-category transfer, not transfer of the refusal direction.",
    "test_reason": "The paper borrows the difference-in-means steering recipe from Arditi et al. to build five virtue-minus-vice Moral Foundations vectors. It injects them, gated by a probe (AMF), and reports its own defense's HarmBench ASR, XSTest over-refusal and capability scores. It never extracts the harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, ablates it, or adds it. Its 'deconstructing the monolithic refusal vector' framing is a design motivation, not a test of V4. The cross-lingual results concern moral-category probes, not refusal-direction transfer (V6).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that moral concepts are linearly separable in middle layers, with a shared but culturally distinct English/Chinese subspace. They report that Moral Vectors give causal control over moral inclination, and that AMF reduces HarmBench ASR while minimizing XSTest over-refusal at little utility cost. They make no claim about the original refusal direction.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The original method uses a harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction and tests projection or addition. This paper extracts five virtue-minus-vice moral-foundation vectors, injects them at Layer 17, and combines them with a probe-based adaptive gate; it does not ablate the refusal direction.",
    "method_sensitivity": "AMF results depend on the gating threshold τ; the paper reports τ = 0.2 as the operating point minimizing both HarmBench ASR and XSTest refusal rate, with lower thresholds described as too aggressive and higher thresholds as too conservative.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Probe-gated injection of moral-foundation vectors (AMF) lowers HarmBench ASR. On Qwen3-8B it drops from 39.67% to 21.33%; on Llama it reaches 19.66%. It also lowers XSTest over-refusal (Qwen 12.80%→5.60%; Llama 2.00%). A static Global Virtue Vector gives smaller gains.",
        "proposed_claim": "Adding non-refusal concept directions (virtue-minus-vice) can induce refusals on harmful prompts and reduce over-refusal on benign ones.",
